of course not everyone has been able to  go down that path in the four months  that Kovac 19 is upended our lives  physically it's also had a tremendous  impact on us psychologically a poll by  Angus Reed found 31 percent of Canadians  said they feel worn-out and fatigued 28  percent feel anxious 22 percent said  they're worried and only 16 percent said  they feel normal  what is all this stress doing to our  brains  Susan wit Bourne is here with some  insight she's a professor of  psychological and brain sciences at the  University of Massachusetts and she  joins us from just outside Boston hi  there  you know I've heard lots of anecdotes  about people being tired and distracted  and having trouble sleeping but what is  this pandemic actually doing to our  brains and are we wired to cope with a  crisis that has no end in sight this is  the most unusual of stressful events  perhaps ever although of course there  have been previous pandemics and there  have been Wars I mean there's all kinds  of traumatic events people have lived  through this one is different because it  is global it is uncertain in terms of  its ending and it leaves us with really  feeling very depleted in our ability to  cope so is there such a thing as crisis  fatigue or Quarantine fatigue in' and  how does it manifest itself in our  brains in our bodies we've never seen  this kind of fatigue there's something  called compassion fatigue which we're  seeing of course in healthcare workers  and mental health workers as well it's  just a really strong source of burnout  but for the average person Quarantine  fatigue is just something we don't even  know what to do with so the natural  response is to give up and I think what  makes that it makes it harder to keep  the energy going to remain vigilant is  that it seems to be getting better in  places that have been practicing good  public health so people say oh well I  don't need to worry them because the  trend is going down but the trend is  going down  because people are complying and when  they don't the trend spike so there's  that and then there's this distance of  yourself from the actual stressor which  again is somewhat similar to war  terrorism and all of the stressful  events that everybody goes through but  this one it unless it struck you or your  family somebody you work with a neighbor  it doesn't feel quite real and the  stress both fear of the virus and and  financial uncertainty for a lot of  people who have lost their jobs can that  affect your cognitive abilities yes it  can cortisol is the stress hormone and  we know that cortisol is supposed to  show sort of a peak in the morning and  then go down throughout the day peak  again on the next morning but what  stress does is it actually ironically  flattens that curve which is not a good  curve to flatten people who are under  prolonged psychological stress show a  flattening of their diurnal or daily  variations in cortisol and that impairs  their health long-term what are some of  the signs that someone is becoming  overwhelmed huge stress manifests as  nightmares even trembling you know a  very low mood you know great deal of  anxiety and so yeah you can easily see  people being stressed but you can also  see it in terms of denial people use  denial when they're stressed it's a  really natural way to kind of fight off  bad news  so you might see denial and that might  be some of the people who sort of  minimize the whole thing and say oh you  know it's nothing or I've survived or I  don't need to worry about this or why  should I worry about this so you can see  both extremes of people's ways of coping  how about the separation from loved ones  who we'd usually turn to in times of  stress does that make things worse and  are there actual physiological effects  from prolonged social isolation yes  there are and there's been a great deal  of concern  a psychological community especially  with older adults which happens to be my  specialty and social isolation among  older adults is a problem in general but  it is exacerbated by the enforced social  isolations on the plus side though  people are resilient and older people  are resilient they've lived through a  lot so people are finding ways to  overcome the social isolation and I  think being very creative about it well  yeah and people are connecting virtually  like we are you know using FaceTime and  Skype and zoom is that really a  replacement for in-person contact or  does her body and our brain respond  differently to connecting that way the  body definitely responds to touching you  know touch has its own host of benefits  so you're missing out on that no denying  it so there's that but then the fact  that people are going out of their way  to surmount the physical distance then  can also be reassuring to people because  you might be getting calls from people  you otherwise wouldn't and maybe even on  a more regular basis because people are  checking in with you to see how you're  doing do you think there could be a  long-term impact from this pandemic on  the psychological health of people I  definitely do and and it's interesting  you asked the question that way because  one of the things that I study is the  effect of kind of contact social context  history on psychological development so  there will be a pre kovat and a post  Kogan reality but humans are social  animals we want to get back together we  want to overcome this and we're  resilient so we're going to see changes  some of them will be bad but some of  them might surprise us because of the  resilience that people show  [Music]
 

